You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput microservice suffers from both memory leaks and failed lookup operations inside a custom caching layer. The microservice uses `ConcurrentHashMap<CacheKey, Value>` to store dynamic cache entries. You discover the following implementation of the `CacheKey` class:

java
public class CacheKey {
    private final String id;
    private List<String> permissions;

    public CacheKey(String id, List<String> permissions) {
        this.id = id;
        this.permissions = permissions;
    }

    public void addPermission(String permission) {
        this.permissions.add(permission);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(id, permissions);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof CacheKey)) return false;
        CacheKey key = (CacheKey) o;
        return Objects.equals(id, key.id) && Objects.equals(permissions, key.permissions);
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where a low-latency trading engine using a custom lock-free ring buffer collection (`CustomRingBuffer<T>`) experiences race conditions, stale reads, and index corruption under multi-threaded producer-consumer workloads:

java
public class CustomRingBuffer<T> {
    private final T[] buffer;
    private long head = 0;
    private long tail = 0;

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public CustomRingBuffer(int capacity) {
        this.buffer = (T[]) new Object[capacity];
    }

    public boolean offer(T item) {
        if (tail - head >= buffer.length) return false;
        buffer[(int) (tail & (buffer.length - 1))] = item;
        tail++;
        return true;
    }

    public T poll() {
        if (head == tail) return null;
        T item = buffer[(int) (head & (buffer.length - 1))];
        head++;
        return item;
    }
}
